Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)
Young Adult & Adolecence
Chronic bloody diarrhoea, Abdominal cramping, Weight loss, Elevated faecal calprotectin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-024
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, bloody diarrhoea accompanied by agonizing abdominal cramping and profound orthostatic weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient experiencing up to 15 episodes of frankly bloody diarrhoea over the past 24 hours, accompanied by severe abdominal pain, low-grade fevers, and inability to maintain hydration. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to evaluate for hemodynamic instability, rule out acute toxic megacolon or bowel perforation, and administer parenteral symptom control prior to the availability of the regular general practitioner.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am passing pure blood and mucus every hour, the cramps are ripping my stomach apart, and I am too weak to stand."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe acute exacerbation of a chronic, progressively worsening bowel habit. For the past three months, the patient has experienced chronic persistent diarrhoea often containing blood or mucus, coupled with recurrent crampy abdominal pain. This has resulted in a 6kg unintentional weight loss and generalized systemic malaise. Over the last 48 hours, the frequency has increased to hourly episodes associated with severe tenesmus (a constant, painful urge to defecate). The patient reports new onset low-grade fevers during this symptomatic flare. The patient also notes several painful, shallow aphthous ulcers in the oral cavity.
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely exhausted, crawling to the adjacent bathroom due to severe weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk for profound hypovolemia and falls; patient is living alone and unable to care for themselves.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 92/58 (Supine) -> 78/48 (Standing) | HR: 118 | Temp: 37.9 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears cachectic, pale, diaphoretic, and in acute distress, holding their abdomen tightly. Capillary refill is delayed (4 seconds).
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals mild distension. There is severe, diffuse lower abdominal tenderness, most prominent in the left lower quadrant. Crucially, there is no board-like rigidity, generalized rebound tenderness, or absent bowel sounds to definitively suggest acute perforation, though the bowel sounds are hyperactive and high-pitched. Oral examination confirms multiple minor aphthous ulcers. Perianal inspection reveals severe excoriation but no frank fistulae. The clinical presentation is highly aligned with the diagnostic tetrad of chronic bloody diarrhoea, abdominal cramping, weight loss, and anticipates a profoundly elevated faecal calprotectin upon laboratory testing.
Environmental Observations: Commode is heavily soiled with gross blood and mucus; patient is clearly unable to maintain hygiene due to the frequency of bowel movements.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Exacerbation of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Suspected Ulcerative Colitis) with Hypovolaemia - Status: Medical Emergency
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Dysentery (e.g., Shigella) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause due to the 3-month chronicity of symptoms and significant weight loss prior to this acute hyper-flare, though stool cultures are required.

Ischaemic Colitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's young age, lack of cardiovascular risk factors, and the presence of systemic inflammatory signs like aphthous ulcers.

Irritable Bowel Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of gross hematochezia, fever, marked weight loss, and objective orthostatic hypotension, which definitively indicate organic, destructive pathology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Severe functional impairment, living alone)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe flare of suspected inflammatory bowel disease characterized by intractable bloody diarrhoea, significant abdominal cramping, and objective hemodynamic instability (orthostatic hypotension and resting tachycardia). The sheer volume of fluid and blood loss has led to critical hypovolaemia. Delaying assessment until the next morning would have predictably resulted in cardiovascular collapse or allowed the potential progression to toxic megacolon. The urgent after-hours home visit confirmed severe systemic compromise and the absence of a surgically rigid abdomen, mandating immediate escalation to the Emergency Department for intravenous fluid resuscitation, high-dose intravenous corticosteroids, and urgent gastroenterology review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate escalation to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000 called by clinician). Advised receiving ED of suspected acute severe IBD flare with hypovolaemia, requiring urgent IV hydration, FBC, CRP, stool cultures, and faecal calprotectin, prior to inpatient colonoscopy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent syncopal collapse before the ambulance arrives, lie strictly flat on the floor with your legs elevated on a pillow, directly forcing blood flow back to your brain and heart. To allow the hospital to accurately assess the severity of the bleeding, do not flush the toilet or clean the commode further, providing the medical team with vital visual evidence of the exact volume and type of blood loss.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None. Immediate ED transfer.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Post-discharge multi-disciplinary review involving GP, gastroenterologist, and IBD specialist nurse.

